-- Section 1: Weight Memory --

  Weight memory = params * bits / 8. This sets how big a model fits and how many
  bytes the bandwidth-bound decode step must read per token.

         variant      GB
            FP16   14.00
             FP8    7.00
     INT8 (W8A8)    7.00
      INT4 (AWQ)    3.50
  [check] memory shrinks monotonically as bit-width falls

-- Section 2: Decode Latency --

  Decode reads the full weight set per token, so per-token time ~ weight bytes /
  bandwidth. Lower precision => fewer bytes => proportionally faster decode.

         variant   ms/token   speedup
            FP16      51.85     1.00x
             FP8      25.93     2.00x
     INT8 (W8A8)      25.93     2.00x
      INT4 (AWQ)      12.96     4.00x
  [check] lower precision speeds up bandwidth-bound decode

-- Section 3: Pareto Frontier + SLA Pick --

  Plot accuracy-drop (lower better) against latency (lower better). A variant is
  on the frontier if nothing else is better on BOTH axes. Pick by the binding
  SLA: max acceptable accuracy drop and max acceptable latency.

  Pareto-optimal variants: FP16, FP8, INT4 (AWQ)
  SLA [tight quality, loose latency  ] -> FP8
  SLA [loose quality, tight latency  ] -> INT4 (AWQ)
  [check] SLA selects the right precision from the frontier

